@vsm 1.0
// EXP-CORE-01/spec.vsm — contractive-core calibration: measure θ_A, L, and the support of μ*
// domain_context: {target=vsf-nucleus, env=analysis, layer=L+2}
// date: 2026-06-15 | status: PRE-REGISTERED (hypotheses fixed before running)
// suite: certifier-validation (with EXP-VNC-01, EXP-G-01) | binary: core_calibration (main.rs, TBD)

spec(EXP-CORE-01, "calibrate the contractive core Ω_A: θ_A, operator norm L, and μ* support"):

  motivation:
    "The hybrid-invariance theorem (paper §3) is stated over Ω_A = {cos≥θ_A} with operator norm
     L<1, but θ_A=0.88 and L≈0.85 are IMPORTED (Fierro 2026-05-13 derivation), not measured in the
     paper. Two consequences a reviewer (Fierro) will press: (1) the theorem's central hypothesis
     rests on an imported constant; (2) the declared tension L≈0.85 (full-state Jacobian, cos metric)
     vs λ_max=ln0.99 (reduced 4-state map) is two operators — only λ_max is measured. EXP-NS-01 also
     shows the noisy steady state LEAKS below cos=0.88 (min_cos: 0.871 at σ=0.15, 0.856 at σ=2.0),
     which sits uneasily with the claim that the invariant measure μ* is 'supported on Ω_A'.
     EXP-CORE-01 measures all three: θ_A, L on the FULL-state map, and the actual support of μ*."

  model:
    local_contraction:
      "ρ(s) = one-step contraction ratio at cos-level s: place two nearby states whose cos to the
       attractor is s, evolve one tick of the full-state map f₀, measure d(f₀x₁,f₀x₂)/d(x₁,x₂) in the
       cosine metric. Sweep s from 1.00 down to θ_viable=0.70. ρ<1 = contraction."
    measured_boundary:
      "θ_A_measured = the largest s below which ρ(s)≥1 (contraction fails). Compare to imported 0.88."
    operator_norm:
      "L_measured = sup_{s∈Ω_A} ρ(s) — the operator norm of the FULL-state map on the core (the
       operator the theorem actually uses). Compare to imported 0.85 AND to the reduced-map λ_max:
       states explicitly which operator gives which number, resolving the declared tension by measurement."
    measure_support:
      "From sustained-noise runs (EXP-NS-01 style), the fraction of steady-state time spent in Ω_A
       vs Ω∖Ω_A, as a function of σ — the measured 'concentration' of μ*."

  hypotheses (pre-registered):
    H_CORE1: "ρ(s)<1 (contraction) for s above some θ_A_measured, with ρ rising as s falls. θ_A_measured
              is reported and compared to the imported 0.88 (may agree, may not — pre-registered open)."
    H_CORE2: "L_measured = sup ρ on Ω_A < 1 — the theorem's hypothesis holds for the full-state map,
              not only the reduced map. The L-vs-λ tension becomes a measured statement of which
              operator/subspace gives which contraction number."
    H_CORE3: "μ* concentrates in Ω_A at low noise (fraction in Ω_A → 1 as σ→0) and leaks below θ_A at
              high σ — quantifying the support claim honestly. Replaces 'supported on Ω_A' with a
              measured concentration profile."

  deliverable:
    "ρ(cos) contraction curve → measured θ_A and L; μ* concentration vs σ. Closes the imported-constants
     debt and converts the L-vs-λ tension into a measured statement. The calibration counterpart of the
     trilogy: where the geometry trilogy measured the attractor, EXP-CORE measures the core the THEOREM
     is stated over."

  paper_impact:
    "§3 — Def(Ω_A): θ_A measured not imported; the L-vs-λ remark backed by measurement.
     §Limitations — the 'residual assumptions' item (Ω_A invariance) upgraded from asserted to
     partially measured (μ* concentration). Directly answers the anticipated Fierro objection."

  implementation_notes:
    "Reuse the lyapunov_spectrum perturbation/placement machinery + the basin/stasis cos metric. The
     cos-level placement = perturb the attractor along random directions scaled to hit target s. μ*
     support reuses sustained_noise. Analysis-only; operational kernel path untouched."

